## Part 2.0 Takeaways

> [!check] Key concepts to internalize
>
> - **Freedom is a coordinate, not a number:** a position on two axes (what you protect, and for how long), not a FIRE figure.
> - **Four layers of spending:** Essential (Shield), Foundational (Engine), Executional (Catalyst), Exponential (Multiplier). Each is an investment on a different timeline.
> - **Runway is measured by layer:** "save 3–6 months" is incomplete, because expenses are four things. Stage 4 (1 year of E+F) is the first real freedom milestone.
> - **Move right, from the layers that matter:** the goal is extending coverage, not maximising a balance.
> - **Three rules:** layers compound at different speeds; the Ratchet (runway never falls); the Floor Lever (every RM1 off Essential compounds for decades).
> - **Split the Exponential layer:** a ring-fenced Compounding Baseline you never touch, and an Innovation Fund you are free to Kelly-bet only because the baseline is safe.

## Your Coordinate Task List

Plot yourself before you read on.

1. **Tag every expense to a layer** — sort your spending into Essential, Foundational, Executional, Exponential. Most people have never separated the Engine from the Shield.
2. **Find your current runway by layer** — how many months of E+F could you cover today if income stopped? That is your horizontal position.
3. **Name your Essential floor** — and one realistic move that lowers it (the Floor Lever is your highest-leverage play).
4. **Ring-fence the baseline** — split your Exponential layer into Compounding Baseline (untouchable) and Innovation Fund (swing here).
5. **Set the next checkpoint** — pick the next stage to the right (most readers are aiming at Stage 3 or Stage 4) and sequence income toward it.

